{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791695991,"nonce":"LrJ5q7ZwBoKi50AdLx9x+IIoFtE=","title":"Several texts in a list item were laid out as a line each","message":"An item made of more than one text, as a framework makes `{index}. {label}`, showed \"1\", \".\", and \"One\" on three lines. Only an element that holds an inline element or a br was a flow; one that held only text was plain text, which is right for one text and wrong for several in an item, whose column stacked them.\n\nFix: an element of the flow tags that holds more than one text with something in it is a flow too, so the texts make one line and wrap together. A text going empty or coming back is a change to that, so a text's data change touches its parent. tests/native-lists.mjs has an item of pieces, one going empty and back, and requires one line.\n\nNot covered: a div or span of several texts, which are not flow tags, so their texts still sit side by side as flex items, with the edge spaces of each text lost.","files":null}]}